# Cross-environment registry strategy.
#
# We deliberately do NOT pin a `registry=` here — that would override your
# ~/.npmrc and force every dev onto one host. Instead we set:
#
#   replace-registry-host=always
#
# which tells npm: when reading `resolved` URLs from package-lock.json,
# replace the host with whatever registry is currently configured (via
# ~/.npmrc, npm_config_registry env var, or npm's built-in default).
#
# Net effect:
#   - Internal dev (Databricks laptop): your ~/.npmrc points at the
#     corporate proxy. npm fetches from there; lock URLs get rewritten
#     to proxy host as needed. No code changes.
#   - GitHub Actions / external CI: no ~/.npmrc, so npm uses its default
#     public registry. Lock URLs (which may carry the proxy host from
#     internal dev) get rewritten to npmjs.org. No workflow changes.
#
# One package-lock.json works in both environments.
replace-registry-host=always
